      "writtenOn": "2017-03-22T02:21:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m worried about the possible consequences of leaving things in a semi-initialized state here. Note that we can\u0027t piggy-back off the fact that the |wifiEnabled| check seems to be safe (no observed problems with it), since we skip much more code than that check does.\n\nPart of me wants to argue that \"the right\" fix is to update SystemServer.java, so that we don\u0027t even bother trying to start Wifi (or, perhaps: any of networking), in this pre-decrypt state.\n\nI think we could accomplish that by changing SystemServer.startOtherServices() to modify the initialization of |disableNetwork| as follows:\n\n  boolean disableNetwork \u003d SystemProperties.getBoolean(\"config.disable_network\", false) || mOnlyCore;\n\nThis approach would have three benefits:\n- ensures that no one can get a reference to WifiServiceImpl, and thereby cause the Impl to crash (which would also crash system server)\n- if encryption changes in the future, we won\u0027t need to make further changes to WifiServiceImpl (we can piggy-back on the mOnlyCore logic in SystemServer)\n- might speed up booting, since we\u0027d only start the network services once, instead of twice\n\nThat said, I do see an argument that the window of time that we\u0027re in this semi-initialized state is small. And I can imagine at least the possibility that not starting network services at all will cause other things to break. (i.e., some things might break worse with the not-running-service, than with the semi-initialized-service.)\n\nWhat do you think?",
      "revId": "e0b9d83108aa35004541c84bc57e4ee10f81b775",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
